<Base title="About" description="What this site is, where the material came from, and why it tells you.">
  <h1>Built, not found</h1>

  <p>
    Most belief systems present their origin as discovery rather than construction. Fractalism takes
    the opposite position, on purpose: <strong>this was built</strong> — assembled, iteratively, out
    of mathematics that's real, psychology that's tested, and stories people already found true
    enough to love. Saying so isn't a confession. It's the method, stated once, applied to itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reasoning is simple: a belief that requires you not to look too closely at its own foundation
    is teaching you, quietly, not to look too closely at anything.
    <a href="/node/belief-systems-built/">The full argument is here.</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What this site is</h2>
  <p>
    A living home for the complete Fractalism handbook — every chapter, unabridged, free — plus the
    interactive pieces the text always implied: the <a href="/map/">relational map</a>, the
    <a href="/thirds/">Standing Thirds gallery</a>, and the <a href="/weaving/">Weaving</a> practice.
    “Living” means it keeps being revised; the <a href="/iterations/">Iterations</a> page records
    every change, because a text about revision should be visibly revisable.
  </p>

  <h2>What it asks of you</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing. No account, no email, no donation, no declaration. If a practice here is useful, use it.
    If a name for a pattern helps you see the pattern, keep the name. If none of it lands, the source
    library below is full of excellent books that stand entirely on their own.
  </p>

  <h2>The sources</h2>
  <p>
    Fractalism cites its raw material openly — the complete list is in the
    <a href="/node/sources/">Source Library</a>, from <em>Thinking in Systems</em> to
    <em>Thinking, Fast and Slow</em> to <em>Atomic Habits</em> to <em>Cat's Cradle</em>.
    Where a piece of this framework resembles something an older tradition already recognized,
    the text credits the resemblance directly rather than hiding it.
  </p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>
    The entire site — text, code, and the graph that connects it — is public at
    <a href="https://github.com/SeantheBomb/fractalist-system">github.com/SeantheBomb/fractalist-system</a>.
    Found an error, a better formulation, or a pattern worth standing up? That's not a deviation
    from Fractalism. <a href="/node/open-questions/">Building it is Fractalism, applied to itself.</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    No cookies, no accounts, no tracking identifiers. The site counts anonymous page visits and which
    page led to which, to learn which threads people actually follow — nothing that identifies you.
    Your reading thread and any Weavings you draw live only in your own browser.
  </p>
</Base>
